* Fri Oct 16 2026 Jeremy Melanson <1080872+zish@users.noreply.github.com> - 1.0.5-1
- Exit when the session that started this daemon is gone, instead of polling an
  empty binder for ever. container_manager.stop() already means to kill us at
  exactly that point and silently cannot: its `pidof waydroid-sensord` runs
  confined as waydroid_t, SELinux denies that domain read on /proc itself
  (tclass=dir, name="/", ino=1), so pidof enumerates nothing, matches nothing
  and exits 1 -- and check=False plus `if pid:` turn a hard failure into a
  no-op. Four session stops in waydroid.log, four empty pidof results, and
  `% kill` appears nowhere in the file (docs/70).
- Not cosmetic. libgbinder answers a dead service manager by polling handle 0
  for ever, and every probe of a binder nobody owns costs two kernel log lines.
  One leaked daemon wrote 20,031 of them in under three hours -- 81.9% of that
  boot's binder output -- and the gap has no upper bound, because it closes only
  when somebody logs in again. A tablet left at the greeter pings all night.
- The question is asked over id.waydro.Container's GetSession, which costs no
  binder traffic, and asked REPEATEDLY rather than once: stop() clears its
  session as the last statement of its try, measured 6 s after the service
  manager died, so a single check at death time would always see a live session
  and stay up for ever. Polls every 2 s, gives up after 60 s, and past that
  behaves exactly as before -- so a framework restart under a live session still
  re-registers both names.
- Fails toward staying up on any bus error, because the two mistakes are not
  symmetric: a daemon that wrongly stays up only writes log noise, which is
  today's behaviour, while one that wrongly exits takes the sensors and the
  backlight with it until the next login.
- The backlight restore moves ahead of that decision rather than sitting beside
  it, so docs/37's black screen -- panel at 0 behind the greeter -- cannot
  become a function of how long this process happens to live.
- Adds two DT_NEEDED entries, libgio-2.0.so.0 and libgobject-2.0.so.0. The daemon was already
  linked against both; --as-needed had been dropping them because no symbol from either was used.
  Both ship in glib2, which this package already Requires and which bigtab01 already has, so
  nothing new is pulled in -- compared against the installed 1.0.4 binary rather than assumed.

* Thu Oct 15 2026 Jeremy Melanson <1080872+zish@users.noreply.github.com> - 1.0.4-1
- Add --idle-probe, which subscribes and unsubscribes sensors directly through
  activate() and batch(), runs the real main loop between steps, and reports
  the poll timer's state, the number of times PollOnce ran and the CPU each
  phase cost. No binder, no container, no service registration, so it does not
  disturb a running daemon. It exits non-zero if the timer stops following what
  is subscribed.
- This exists because the claim that an idle daemon does not wake up at all was
  unverifiable on a running system: two framework listeners hold the
  accelerometer for the life of the session, so no zero-subscriber window ever
  occurs. Measured now: nothing subscribed means the timer is off, PollOnce
  runs exactly 0 times, and the process spends 0.2 ms over 4 seconds.
- Add SensorIIO::Polls() and Samples() counters to support that. A CPU figure
  alone cannot settle it, since 0.00%% and 0.4%% of a core both read as small.
- No change to any behaviour the daemon exhibits while serving Android. The two
  counter increments are the only code reached on the normal path.
- Corrects 1.0.3's note on buffered mode. That entry cited magn_3d delivering
  10.3 Hz against 10 Hz advertised; re-measured, magn_3d gives 0.00-0.17 Hz and
  the earlier figure has not reproduced. What does hold, and is new, is that
  accel_3d pushes at 44.66 Hz, dev_rotation at 30.99 Hz and gyro_3d at
  20.16 Hz, all with no sysfs write at all -- faster than Android asks for in
  every case. Push mode is nonetheless not being adopted; see
  docs/67-sensor-poll-cost.md for the numbers and for the kernel divide-by-zero
  that writing a hysteresis attribute can trigger on this hub.

* Wed Oct 14 2026 Jeremy Melanson <1080872+zish@users.noreply.github.com> - 1.0.3-1
- Stop using in_<type>_sampling_frequency to pace polled reads. 1.0.2 treated it
  as the rate beyond which a read would return the previous sample, and that is
  wrong: a _raw read issues an explicit HID GET_REPORT and the hub answers with
  a freshly converted sample. Polling accel_3d at 40 Hz with the machine
  demonstrably still -- median step 2-3 LSB, total range under 20 LSB against a
  4-count LSB -- returns fresh data 97.9%% of the time, and at 20 Hz 100%% of the
  time, against an attribute that reads 10 Hz.
- So 1.0.2 did not reclaim free work on the accelerometer, it under-sampled it,
  and feeding the same figure into minDelay told Android 10 Hz was the ceiling
  when the framework had been asking for 66.67 ms (SENSOR_DELAY_UI) and the
  hardware serves it comfortably. minDelay is back to the poll_hz ceiling.
- The attribute does govern the unsolicited-report path, which is what buffered
  mode on magn_3d showed: 10.3 Hz delivered against 10 Hz advertised. It is
  still read and logged for that reason, and for a future push-mode reader, but
  it no longer influences any poll period.
- Everything else from 1.0.2 stands and is unaffected: honouring batch(),
  stopping the timer when nothing is subscribed, sharing the dev_rotation read
  between ORIENTATION and ROTATION_VECTOR, held fds with pread, and repeat
  suppression behind a 1 s heartbeat. Those were measured independently.

* Wed Oct 14 2026 Jeremy Melanson <1080872+zish@users.noreply.github.com> - 1.0.2-1
- Poll each sensor at its own rate instead of all of them at a flat 20 Hz.
  in_<type>_sampling_frequency says the hub publishes accel_3d and magn_3d at
  10 Hz, so half of every accelerometer poll was three HID round trips fetching
  bytes the hardware had not refreshed. Measured 1.9%% of a core down to 0.7%%
  with the same sensors subscribed. docs/67-sensor-poll-cost.md.
- Honour ISensors::batch(). The requested sampling period was being read off the
  parcel into a throwaway and discarded, so a client asking SENSOR_DELAY_NORMAL
  was served four times faster than it wanted. maxReportLatencyNs stays ignored
  on purpose: the sensor list advertises fifoMaxEventCount = 0.
- Advertise minDelay per sensor rather than from poll_hz alone. Claiming 20 Hz
  on a node that publishes at 10 only made Android ask for round trips that
  returned the same bytes; it now selects 100 ms where it used to pick 66.67.
- Stop the poll timer when nothing is subscribed. It was added in the Sensors
  constructor and never removed, so an idle daemon still woke 20 times a second.
- Read dev_rotation once per tick and share it. ORIENTATION and ROTATION_VECTOR
  are the same quaternion from the same attribute of the same node, so with both
  enabled it cost a second 1.15 ms i2c round trip for a report already in hand.
- Suppress repeat samples, with a 1 s heartbeat floor so a sensor advertised as
  continuous cannot fall silent on a motionless machine. This also makes the
  existing `ts != last_TimeStamp` guard work: every read used to be stamped with
  the current time, so the two values differed by construction.
- Hold one fd per attribute and pread instead of fopen/fclose on every poll:
  0.097 ms to 0.058 ms of CPU per attribute read, measured. The fds are dropped
  only from the main thread -- closing them from an hwbinder thread races with a
  pread in progress, and the bad outcome is a recycled fd number reading another
  file rather than EBADF.
- No change to the axis conventions, scale factors, warm-up windows or the
  ILight half.

* Thu Sep 24 2026 Jeremy Melanson <1080872+zish@users.noreply.github.com> - 1.0.1-1
- Log each brightness change at INFO rather than DEBUG, so the one measurement
  in bin/brightness-test.sh that does not depend on Android's display policy
  can run on the real host. container_manager.py spawns this daemon with a
  hardcoded argv and no --verbose, so at DEBUG the line was unreachable in
  production and the mapping check had never once run there.
- No other behaviour changes. The line lands in /var/lib/waydroid/waydroid.log,
  where container_manager.py already captures this daemon's stderr, and costs
  one line per brightness change against the lxc-info poll written to the same
  file every 2 s.

* Thu Sep 24 2026 Jeremy Melanson <1080872+zish@users.noreply.github.com> - 1.0.0-1
- First package. The host-side sensors daemon, which until now was hand-copied
  to /usr/local/bin and owned by nothing.
- Unblocks waydroid-ext-backlight, which grants an SELinux permission to this
  daemon by name and so installed inert while no package shipped it.
- Carries a %%bcond for the prebuilt binary. The default is the real source
  build; the dev box is Debian and cannot compile against Fedora's
  libgbinder-devel, so an installable RPM is produced here with
  `packaging/build-mod.sh --prebuilt sensord`.
